Z||000002||FieroMaster88||05-03-2001||10:45 PM||||Ok 1Fst, What type of shop is this???  I want to start a preformance shop, am almost done with school, just have to take my ASE tests, but I was wondering what else soemone needs to do that type of stuff?  Just need an idea.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">        <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||05-04-2001||08:31 AM||||Reliable Automotive was started 10 years ago as a basic service shop and once an establish client base was set the Performance Side of the Shop ( Engineered Performance ) was opened with the purchase of the dyno. there is not a whole lot of money in Performance work. It's difficult to sell -fast-. everyone wants it but it's NOT cheap. thier car has to run perfect THEN you can make it fast.. many customers just want fast now.. not after the headlights are functioning and the shocks and struts replaced... a fast car is nothing if it planted in the wall casue of faulty equipment.  the few that understand  this are usually willing to pony up big bucks.. you just need great customer service and if you say the car will be done on a date/time make SURE it is.. that's the biggest way to drive a customer away. the shop was started by 2 ASE Master Techs both with ME Degrees from Ga. Tech (thats how i met them -through school) so fabrication and knowing why/how things work is always a plus to drum up business. the ownership of the shop was changed on march 1st From Ed (not the shop Dyno tuner) to the owner and founder of <A HREF="http://www.enthuza.com" TARGET=_blank>www.enthuza.com</A>  Chad Sanders.. he's got the  patience to deal with customers and the marketing know-how needed to make the shop way more successful. And now Ed is able to dedecate his time to what he likes to do.. Tune and fabricate custom set-ups, not worry about $ and hope that the guys in the office are doing their job.  I guess what i'm saying is.. find a tunign shop and work their.. it's really difficult to start your own! Blue Water Hot Rods in Port Huron is a pretty decent shop (not dyno equipped but still nice) ||||reg||1
